## Statement refuted

Every finite-dimensional supermodule over every finite-dimensional complex Lie superalgebra is semisimple.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The module $K(0,0)$, with $v$ even and $w$ odd.

[F1] [[def-gl-1-1-bracket-data]]: $\mathfrak{gl}(1|1)$ has the displayed four-dimensional graded basis and bracket table.

[F2] [[def-two-dimensional-highest-weight-module-for-gl-1-1]]: the action on $K(0,0)$ is the specialization of the displayed family.

[F3] [[thm-two-dimensional-highest-weight-module-for-gl-1-1-is-well-defined]]: this action satisfies the $\mathfrak{gl}(1|1)$-supermodule identity.

[F4] [[def-simple-and-semisimple-lie-supermodules]]: semisimple means an internal direct sum of simple graded submodules.

[F5] [[thm-gl-1-1-is-a-lie-superalgebra]]: the bracket data define a complex Lie superalgebra.

## Counterexample

Take $\mathfrak g=\mathfrak{gl}(1|1)$ and the finite-dimensional module $K(0,0)=\mathbb C v\oplus\mathbb C w$ from [[def-two-dimensional-highest-weight-module-for-gl-1-1]] and [[thm-two-dimensional-highest-weight-module-for-gl-1-1-is-well-defined]]. Its action is $h_1v=h_2v=0$, $h_1w=-w$, $h_2w=w$, $ev=0$, $ew=0$, $fv=w$, and $fw=0$. The line $\mathbb Cw$ and the quotient $K(0,0)/\mathbb Cw$ are one-dimensional simple supermodules, but the extension does not split.

**Proof technique:** direct.


1.1 The subspace $\mathbb Cw$ is a graded submodule: it is preserved by the diagonal operators $h_1,h_2$, while $ew=fw=0$. It is simple because it is one-dimensional and nonzero. The quotient line generated by $v+\mathbb Cw$ is also simple: $h_1,h_2,e,f$ all act by zero on it. [F1, F2, F3, F4, given]


1.2 The only graded complement to $\mathbb Cw$ would have even part $\mathbb Cv$ and zero odd part, since $K(0,0)_{\bar0}=\mathbb Cv$ and $K(0,0)_{\bar1}=\mathbb Cw$. But $f v=w\notin\mathbb Cv$, so this complement is not a submodule. Thus the exact sequence with submodule $\mathbb Cw$ and quotient $K(0,0)/\mathbb Cw$ does not split. [F1, F2, F3, given, algebra]


2.1 Any nonzero graded submodule containing an even vector contains $v$, hence also $fv=w$, and equals the whole module; a nonzero graded submodule with zero even part is exactly $\mathbb Cw$. Thus $\mathbb Cw$ is the only simple graded submodule, and its direct sums cannot equal the $1|1$-dimensional $K(0,0)$. By [F4], this module is not semisimple; [F5] confirms it is an instance covered by the refuted claim. [F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
